Aerie-Purpose: While the user is opted in to Widevine, send only the Widevine component's update traffic to Google; otherwise keep Helium's dummy origin.
Aerie-Upstream-Area: chrome/browser/component_updater/chrome_component_updater_configurator.cc (UpdateUrl, PingUrl), components/update_client/{configurator.h,update_checker.cc,ping_manager.cc}
Aerie-Kind: hook

Helium's component-updates patch sends every component update and ping to its services
origin, which Aerie turned off (services/0002), so the component updater has only the
dummy origin and the Widevine component cannot be downloaded. UpdateUrl() and PingUrl()
now go through aerie_widevine::WidevineUpdateUrl(), which returns Google's update server
while aerie.widevine.enabled is true and the value Helium computed otherwise. Google's
server is the only one that hosts the Widevine component.

Google's server must see nothing but Widevine. The update client has one server for all
components, so update_client::Configurator gets a new ShouldSendToServer(app_id) (default:
true). The update check and the event ping leave an app out when it returns false.
ChromeConfigurator answers through aerie_widevine::ShouldSendToUpdateServer(): while
opted in only the Widevine app id is sent, so the CRLSet is never named to Google and
gets no update check, ping or event. Opted out nothing is held back, because the request
goes to the dummy origin and is refused.

